WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.170
&gt;&gt; Hello everyone. Welcome to

00:00:06.171 --> 00:00:08.540
the final intern talk today.

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:12.395
Today, we have Ben who was
working with us all summer.

00:00:12.396 --> 00:00:14.270
Worked really hard
on a new problem.

00:00:14.271 --> 00:00:16.710
So, it's my pleasure to

00:00:16.711 --> 00:00:19.815
introduce Ben who's
a PhD student at CMU.

00:00:19.816 --> 00:00:23.990
He works under the supervision
of Professor Bigsha Raj.

00:00:23.991 --> 00:00:26.490
His current research
is focused on machine

00:00:26.491 --> 00:00:29.070
learning for Audio and previously

00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:31.410
he was a staff researcher at

00:00:31.411 --> 00:00:35.220
ICSI at UC Berkley and
he's done a lot of work

00:00:35.221 --> 00:00:39.030
on Audio understanding and

00:00:39.031 --> 00:00:42.140
multimedia problems in
the past and today,

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:44.930
he's going to talk about
his final internship project on

00:00:44.931 --> 00:00:46.865
a cross-modal Audio search engine

00:00:46.866 --> 00:00:49.520
based on joint
audio text embedding.

00:00:49.521 --> 00:00:52.210
So, with that please welcome Ben.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.630
&gt;&gt; Thank you Shuayb for
the introduction and thank you

00:00:56.631 --> 00:00:59.000
everyone for coming here,

00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:02.680
I know that everyone is busy
so I appreciate your time.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.765
So, let's start with

00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:11.155
presenting and
introducing the topic.

00:01:11.156 --> 00:01:12.830
First, we have that there are

00:01:12.831 --> 00:01:15.935
many multimedia and
audio recordings

00:01:15.936 --> 00:01:18.070
that are captured every day.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.230
All of these recordings carry

00:01:21.231 --> 00:01:23.800
information specifically
in this talk,

00:01:23.801 --> 00:01:25.740
we're talking about
the acoustic information.

00:01:25.741 --> 00:01:27.745
There are sounds, soundscapes,

00:01:27.746 --> 00:01:29.130
there's information that tell us

00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:31.610
what's going on in
this recordings.

00:01:31.611 --> 00:01:34.030
There are recordings
that are captured

00:01:34.031 --> 00:01:35.870
and shared in
social media, for example,

00:01:35.871 --> 00:01:38.840
where people typically
will have some words or

00:01:38.841 --> 00:01:42.265
descriptions to describe what's
going on in the content.

00:01:42.266 --> 00:01:45.650
But, on the other hand, there's
also recordings like from

00:01:45.651 --> 00:01:50.255
Amazon Echo or Alexa
and these devices,

00:01:50.256 --> 00:01:52.065
also surveillance camera,

00:01:52.066 --> 00:01:54.970
customer services,
recordings et cetera,

00:01:54.971 --> 00:01:58.265
where no one takes the
time to label or annotate

00:01:58.266 --> 00:02:02.270
what sounds or what audio
it's in the content.

00:02:02.271 --> 00:02:04.720
So, if we have this recordings,

00:02:04.721 --> 00:02:07.865
it's very complicated to
do search and retrieval.

00:02:07.866 --> 00:02:12.530
So, what people typically
do is to approach

00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:17.240
their problem with either
text or audio similarity.

00:02:17.241 --> 00:02:19.010
So, let's say for
example that we have

00:02:19.011 --> 00:02:20.630
a query on the left and we have

00:02:20.631 --> 00:02:21.980
a pool of information of

00:02:21.981 --> 00:02:24.375
audio and associated
labels on the right.

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:27.625
If we take the query,
that X Query cat,

00:02:27.626 --> 00:02:30.050
we're going to be able to
retrieve the first and

00:02:30.051 --> 00:02:33.670
the third recording because
they're properly labeled.

00:02:33.671 --> 00:02:36.245
In the same way, if we

00:02:36.246 --> 00:02:38.240
have an audio that
corresponds to cat,

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:43.610
we can retrieve the first and
the third and the last one,

00:02:43.611 --> 00:02:45.850
even though the last one
doesn't have the level of cat,

00:02:45.851 --> 00:02:48.575
but acoustically
they are similar.

00:02:48.576 --> 00:02:53.600
On the other hand, if
we pass an audio and

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:55.790
then we are able to

00:02:55.791 --> 00:02:58.670
retrieve the green algae
from the pool,

00:02:58.671 --> 00:03:01.410
but then in this case we
don't have tags, right?

00:03:01.411 --> 00:03:03.190
So, in this case, we use
acoustic similarity,

00:03:03.191 --> 00:03:05.365
but we don't have tag
associated to that sound.

00:03:05.366 --> 00:03:07.400
So, we don't know what
is the content of

00:03:07.401 --> 00:03:10.000
the sound until we
actually listen to it.

00:03:10.001 --> 00:03:12.575
This might be easy for
certain sounds like

00:03:12.576 --> 00:03:14.720
God where there are
some other acoustics and sounds

00:03:14.721 --> 00:03:16.220
that are more
complicated to decipher

00:03:16.221 --> 00:03:17.990
without some contexts and there's

00:03:17.991 --> 00:03:19.700
also people with
disabilities who cannot

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:22.590
listen to the audio
and infer the label.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.210
So, one solution
that is common in

00:03:26.211 --> 00:03:28.700
the literature is to
generate text metadata for

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:30.620
these audio recordings using

00:03:30.621 --> 00:03:32.690
automatic speech recognition or

00:03:32.691 --> 00:03:33.980
sound event detection that

00:03:33.981 --> 00:03:36.440
is their labeled for

00:03:36.441 --> 00:03:40.310
sounds assigned to segments
of their recording.

00:03:40.311 --> 00:03:44.420
Now, what we propose
here and what

00:03:44.421 --> 00:03:48.420
we tried this summer was
a growth model approach.

00:03:48.421 --> 00:03:53.165
We aim for two main goals
in this approach.

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:54.995
The first one is to map

00:03:54.996 --> 00:03:59.135
audio and text into the
same shared latent space.

00:03:59.136 --> 00:04:02.455
That will benefit
us because then,

00:04:02.456 --> 00:04:04.270
audio and texts
can be comparable.

00:04:04.271 --> 00:04:05.890
So, if we pass a Query,

00:04:05.891 --> 00:04:09.155
that is a text or
an audio recording,

00:04:09.156 --> 00:04:12.320
we can map it to
that joint space and

00:04:12.321 --> 00:04:16.395
find the neighbors that
correspond to that recording.

00:04:16.396 --> 00:04:20.320
The second and most
important I think is that

00:04:20.321 --> 00:04:21.520
the shared space should preserve

00:04:21.521 --> 00:04:24.520
semantic similarity between
the text and the audio.

00:04:24.521 --> 00:04:26.715
So, typically, as I was saying,

00:04:26.716 --> 00:04:29.335
the approaches will be
designed separately,

00:04:29.336 --> 00:04:31.280
text and audio, but here,

00:04:31.281 --> 00:04:35.565
we want to combine both meaning,

00:04:35.566 --> 00:04:39.000
the acoustics similarity and
the semantic similarity.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:42.960
For example, if we have that

00:04:42.961 --> 00:04:46.435
the words "violin breaks"
and "violin plays",

00:04:46.436 --> 00:04:49.240
those two are semantically
speaking in terms of

00:04:49.241 --> 00:04:52.350
language similar because
they refer to a violin.

00:04:52.351 --> 00:04:54.580
But, if we think of
the acoustics of

00:04:54.581 --> 00:04:57.285
the sounds that they will
produce, they're different.

00:04:57.286 --> 00:05:02.605
So, we hope and we aim
that these joint space

00:05:02.606 --> 00:05:04.900
we'll find a middle
point to preserve

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:06.385
that similarity or that text

00:05:06.386 --> 00:05:08.340
and the audio at the same time.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:10.930
Okay, so the rest of the talk is

00:05:10.931 --> 00:05:13.420
about the approach that
we follow to create

00:05:13.421 --> 00:05:15.620
this joint and space and how

00:05:15.621 --> 00:05:18.500
well those joint representations

00:05:18.501 --> 00:05:21.830
will for classification
and also for retrieval.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.930
When it comes to related work,

00:05:25.931 --> 00:05:30.580
we selected mainly three papers
and the first one is,

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:34.390
it described an approach
for search and

00:05:34.391 --> 00:05:37.330
retrieval using text
and audio queries.

00:05:37.331 --> 00:05:41.860
Although this was
a fast approach,

00:05:41.861 --> 00:05:45.520
it was developed in

00:05:45.521 --> 00:05:48.565
an independent way where
text and audio were trained

00:05:48.566 --> 00:05:51.655
separately and then at
some point they will combined to

00:05:51.656 --> 00:05:53.260
assign a score and then

00:05:53.261 --> 00:05:56.410
retrieve information
based on those scopes.

00:05:56.411 --> 00:06:00.065
That approach didn't work
without a vocabulary words.

00:06:00.066 --> 00:06:03.285
Then the second
approach from Malcolm,

00:06:03.286 --> 00:06:06.320
he created a semantic space
with text like

00:06:06.321 --> 00:06:09.565
a hierarchy and then
on the acoustic space,

00:06:09.566 --> 00:06:14.010
they cluster audio and then
they linked those two spaces,

00:06:14.011 --> 00:06:18.230
but this approach, it's hard to

00:06:18.231 --> 00:06:20.390
scale because you need to

00:06:20.391 --> 00:06:24.375
create these associations
and clusters.

00:06:24.376 --> 00:06:26.440
Then, on the third approach,

00:06:26.441 --> 00:06:28.820
we actually look at
computer vision and we found

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:32.020
that there's
some papers that refer

00:06:32.021 --> 00:06:34.550
to image and text embeddings
where they tried to

00:06:34.551 --> 00:06:38.620
approach the problem in
a similar way that we described.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.070
So, the approach that we have

00:06:41.071 --> 00:06:43.220
and that we are going to use

00:06:43.221 --> 00:06:47.285
across this presentation
is the Siamese Network.

00:06:47.286 --> 00:06:50.090
The Siamese Network, the overall

00:06:50.091 --> 00:06:52.680
idea I'll explain more
of the details after.

00:06:52.681 --> 00:06:54.440
The overall idea is
that it will take

00:06:54.441 --> 00:06:57.650
audio and text one on each gap

00:06:57.651 --> 00:07:01.910
and you will take

00:07:01.911 --> 00:07:05.210
positive and negative
pairs and the positive,

00:07:05.211 --> 00:07:06.520
that means that the text and

00:07:06.521 --> 00:07:09.340
the audio correspond
to the same class and

00:07:09.341 --> 00:07:11.440
negative pairs mean
that the text and

00:07:11.441 --> 00:07:14.175
the audio corresponds
to a different class.

00:07:14.176 --> 00:07:17.230
So, we will use these pairs
to train this network.

00:07:17.231 --> 00:07:19.105
Then at the end, at the top,

00:07:19.106 --> 00:07:21.080
the loss function is trying to

00:07:21.081 --> 00:07:24.170
make the two modalities that
correspond to the same class

00:07:24.171 --> 00:07:26.030
to be closer to each other and

00:07:26.031 --> 00:07:27.290
the two modalities
that correspond to

00:07:27.291 --> 00:07:30.179
the same class to be separate.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.220
Then at the embedding stage,

00:07:33.221 --> 00:07:35.355
once we have this
neural network train,

00:07:35.356 --> 00:07:37.640
we will be able to feed

00:07:37.641 --> 00:07:40.670
either audio text and then

00:07:40.671 --> 00:07:43.880
create joined representations
or embeddings.

00:07:43.881 --> 00:07:47.045
So, we can see the output
of those embeddings

00:07:47.046 --> 00:07:52.210
as an intermediate feature
that takes in the audio space.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.780
So, what we typically will feed

00:07:55.781 --> 00:07:58.480
to the neural network
are features.

00:07:58.481 --> 00:07:59.740
In this case, for example,

00:07:59.741 --> 00:08:02.500
we used audio features
which are MFCC,

00:08:02.501 --> 00:08:06.175
it's a good standard feature
for this task.

00:08:06.176 --> 00:08:07.930
Since we needed a vector,

00:08:07.931 --> 00:08:09.940
we basically computed the mean of

00:08:09.941 --> 00:08:11.980
the entire MFCC matrix
corresponding

00:08:11.981 --> 00:08:14.070
to each of the other
recordings and

00:08:14.071 --> 00:08:16.780
then we created
mean vectors across time.

00:08:16.781 --> 00:08:23.380
The similar approach with
the text, we used Word2Vec.

00:08:23.381 --> 00:08:25.820
Word2Vec, if you're not
familiar with that,

00:08:25.821 --> 00:08:27.200
is a technique to convert

00:08:27.201 --> 00:08:29.175
one word into a numerical vector,

00:08:29.176 --> 00:08:33.700
but those vectors preserve
a semantic similarity.

00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:37.010
So, words that are similar
will exist in the vectors with

00:08:37.011 --> 00:08:38.790
the close distance and
words that are different

00:08:38.791 --> 00:08:42.150
will exist further apart.

00:08:42.151 --> 00:08:45.290
So, the dimensionality that

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:48.215
we use for our audio and text
features were 300.

00:08:48.216 --> 00:08:50.270
This is basically the setup

00:08:50.271 --> 00:08:52.855
that we use for most of our work.

00:08:52.856 --> 00:08:55.690
Then the input and

00:08:55.691 --> 00:08:57.740
the configuration of
a Siamese network

00:08:57.741 --> 00:08:58.760
was the following; first,

00:08:58.761 --> 00:09:00.650
we pass the features
as I described before

00:09:00.651 --> 00:09:03.280
and we assign a zero or a one,

00:09:03.281 --> 00:09:07.115
depending if we pass in
negative or positive first.

00:09:07.116 --> 00:09:09.960
Then this network is
basically the same,

00:09:09.961 --> 00:09:11.110
that's why they
call them Siamese,

00:09:11.111 --> 00:09:13.130
it's exactly the same and
they share parameters,

00:09:13.131 --> 00:09:17.235
and it was a simple BNN
and the output was 1,024,

00:09:17.236 --> 00:09:20.740
that is the dimensionality
of our embeddings.

00:09:20.741 --> 00:09:23.650
Then once we have
the embeddings from

00:09:23.651 --> 00:09:26.085
other embedding from text,
this is training time.

00:09:26.086 --> 00:09:30.250
We're going to pass
it to the part where

00:09:30.251 --> 00:09:34.640
we enforce the similarity
between modalities.

00:09:34.641 --> 00:09:36.520
&gt;&gt; So, the parameters you said
in the previous line from

00:09:36.521 --> 00:09:39.620
the final parameters you picked,

00:09:39.621 --> 00:09:41.470
after doing other experiment,

00:09:41.471 --> 00:09:43.450
why did you pick those 1,024?

00:09:43.451 --> 00:09:50.100
Because they performed
best? Why was the network-

00:09:50.101 --> 00:09:51.100
&gt;&gt; Oh yeah. The
network parameters?

00:09:51.101 --> 00:09:54.385
Yes, so I basically
carry on some tuning,

00:09:54.386 --> 00:09:55.790
started with one layer and

00:09:55.791 --> 00:09:59.240
then change in the number of
nodes and number of layers

00:09:59.241 --> 00:10:01.500
in powers of two and after

00:10:01.501 --> 00:10:04.360
spend us a reasonable
of time tuning,

00:10:04.361 --> 00:10:05.700
this was the best performance.

00:10:05.701 --> 00:10:10.030
But there's more
explanation to be done.

00:10:10.031 --> 00:10:14.110
Okay. So, this is

00:10:14.111 --> 00:10:15.130
the most important part of

00:10:15.131 --> 00:10:18.025
the neural network because here,

00:10:18.026 --> 00:10:22.720
the letter to basically
work with this combo,

00:10:22.721 --> 00:10:26.085
the contrastive loss
and the L2 distance.

00:10:26.086 --> 00:10:29.230
The goal of these contrastive
loss is, as I say,

00:10:29.231 --> 00:10:32.850
to enforce that pairs that
correspond to a similar class

00:10:32.851 --> 00:10:35.935
closer and different class
to be separate.

00:10:35.936 --> 00:10:38.445
It does it using

00:10:38.446 --> 00:10:42.590
the part that we see on
the right, which is a margin.

00:10:42.591 --> 00:10:44.180
DW is the distance for

00:10:44.181 --> 00:10:49.140
similarity and when
we have similar data,

00:10:49.141 --> 00:10:50.760
we want the pairs,

00:10:50.761 --> 00:10:53.200
we want them to be close
within the margin,

00:10:53.201 --> 00:10:56.080
M. M is a hyperparameter
that we also tune.

00:10:56.081 --> 00:10:57.890
The problem is that

00:10:57.891 --> 00:11:00.515
this actually didn't
work for our data.

00:11:00.516 --> 00:11:04.270
So, we try some other
similarity techniques

00:11:04.271 --> 00:11:08.290
and distance and also
another loss function.

00:11:08.291 --> 00:11:12.840
So, at the end, the combination
that performed the

00:11:12.841 --> 00:11:14.995
best was the binary cross-entropy

00:11:14.996 --> 00:11:18.285
which also has a binary setup.

00:11:18.286 --> 00:11:21.150
But now, the distance,

00:11:21.151 --> 00:11:25.215
we use the exponential
minus L1 or L2.

00:11:25.216 --> 00:11:27.390
The best one was minus L2.

00:11:27.391 --> 00:11:31.550
Here, you have to think that
the L2 and L1 as they are,

00:11:31.551 --> 00:11:34.450
they have an unbounded
score, right?

00:11:34.451 --> 00:11:36.000
So, if it's very similar,

00:11:36.001 --> 00:11:37.460
then it's a small number and if

00:11:37.461 --> 00:11:39.195
it's very large, it
can grow very high.

00:11:39.196 --> 00:11:41.170
But if we use the
negative exponential,

00:11:41.171 --> 00:11:44.375
then we force the distance
to be a number between,

00:11:44.376 --> 00:11:46.190
remember that if you
look at the plots,

00:11:46.191 --> 00:11:47.395
it look like this, the

00:11:47.396 --> 00:11:49.360
exponential with
a negative inside.

00:11:49.361 --> 00:11:50.660
So, if they are similar,

00:11:50.661 --> 00:11:52.460
we're going to have one
and if they are different,

00:11:52.461 --> 00:11:55.615
it will be a number
close to zero.

00:11:55.616 --> 00:11:58.125
So, now we have
pluralistic framework there.

00:11:58.126 --> 00:12:02.160
This one show the best results

00:12:02.161 --> 00:12:04.620
that I'm going to show
later in a different slide.

00:12:04.621 --> 00:12:07.550
&gt;&gt; What is the role of
the second parameter in BCE?

00:12:07.551 --> 00:12:09.975
The first is the divergence.

00:12:09.976 --> 00:12:12.755
&gt;&gt; The BCE?

00:12:12.756 --> 00:12:20.400
&gt;&gt; Yes. 1 minus y_i
logarithm of 1 minus D_w.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:25.755
&gt;&gt; Remember, we're passing
first with zero and one.

00:12:25.756 --> 00:12:27.655
So, one will take care of

00:12:27.656 --> 00:12:28.920
one class and the other one

00:12:28.921 --> 00:12:30.070
will take care of
the other class.

00:12:30.071 --> 00:12:31.750
&gt;&gt; So, y_i can be zero or one?

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:32.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:32.441 --> 00:12:34.010
&gt;&gt; So, if y_i is zero,

00:12:34.011 --> 00:12:36.480
the first down zero and then
the second last will be

00:12:36.481 --> 00:12:40.500
used and then if y_i
is one, then the-

00:12:40.501 --> 00:12:41.760
&gt;&gt; It's a binary. Okay.

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:43.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I know you

00:12:43.211 --> 00:12:44.825
said it's binary for
both of them, right?

00:12:44.826 --> 00:12:47.460
Because we were with
first zero or one.

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:52.690
&gt;&gt; Can you explain why
do we use exponential?

00:12:52.691 --> 00:12:55.590
Why not hyperbolic tangent,

00:12:55.591 --> 00:12:57.870
tan edge function to
do the compression?

00:12:57.871 --> 00:13:01.430
&gt;&gt;Yes. So, in this case,

00:13:01.431 --> 00:13:04.090
as I was saying, we chose
this one because it

00:13:04.091 --> 00:13:07.070
gives you a number
from one to zero.

00:13:07.071 --> 00:13:10.620
We could also use
the cosine similarity or

00:13:10.621 --> 00:13:12.825
any other combination but

00:13:12.826 --> 00:13:16.265
we just try these
combinations, right?

00:13:16.266 --> 00:13:19.400
But certainly, something
else can be explore for

00:13:19.401 --> 00:13:20.610
the distance and also for

